#  Whale Radar — example environment variables
# These are required ONLY if you self-host and want to use a custom
# Supabase Edge Function for the Hyperliquid cache. The default
# deployment talks to api.hyperliquid.xyz directly and needs nothing here.
#
# For Lovable deployments: do NOT use .env.local — set these in
# Project Settings → Environment Variables instead.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxx.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...

# Express server (server/) — shared bearer token required for all
# protected /api/* routes. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Set this on Railway (server) AND on any client calling non-public routes.
# Public routes (no token needed): /api/health, /api/scan
API_AUTH_TOKEN=replace-with-long-random-string

# NOTE: never expose API_AUTH_TOKEN to the browser. Any VITE_-prefixed value is
# baked into the public JS bundle. Protected /api/* routes must be called from
# server-side code (proxy or edge function) that holds the token.

# ── Nexus Bot execution bridge ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The browser never talks to the Express server directly for bot actions — it
# goes through supabase/functions/nexus-bot-proxy, which holds the real
# token. Set these as SUPABASE SECRETS (supabase secrets set NAME=value),
# never as VITE_ client vars:
#   NEXUS_BOT_API_URL=https://your-express-server.example.com
#   API_AUTH_TOKEN=<same value as server/.env's API_AUTH_TOKEN below>
#
# On the Express server itself (server/.env — never committed, never a
# VITE_ var), set exchange credentials per exchange you want live execution
# on (ccxt-supported ids: binance, okx, hyperliquid, backpack if/when ccxt
# adds it). Only the exchanges you configure are usable — others error
# clearly rather than silently failing:
#   BINANCE_API_KEY=...
#   BINANCE_API_SECRET=...
#   OKX_API_KEY=...
#   OKX_API_SECRET=...
#   OKX_API_PASSPHRASE=...
#
# Dry-run is ON by default. Real orders require BOTH of these — the second
# is deliberately an exact phrase, not a plain boolean, so it can't be
# flipped on by a stray typo:
#   NEXUS_DRY_RUN=false
#   NEXUS_LIVE_TRADING_CONFIRM=I_UNDERSTAND_THE_RISK
#
# Optional tuning:
#   NEXUS_COOLDOWN_MINUTES=15      # server-side post-trade cooldown, per pair
#   NEXUS_GRID_POLL_MS=30000       # grid-maintenance worker poll interval
#
# ── Strategy Trader (freqtrade REST API bridge) — optional, second bot ────────
# Only needed if you're running a freqtrade instance and want whale-radar
# signals forwarded to it. On the Express server (server/.env):
#   FREQTRADE_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080        # freqtrade's own api_server address
#   FREQTRADE_API_USERNAME=...                       # from freqtrade's config.json
#   FREQTRADE_API_PASSWORD=...
#   NEXUS_STRATEGY_MAX_STAKE_USD=500                 # hard per-order cap; default 500 if unset
# Shares the ccxt bot's cooldown lock (NEXUS_COOLDOWN_MINUTES) above, but NOT
# its NEXUS_DRY_RUN/NEXUS_LIVE_TRADING_CONFIRM flags — whether a forceenter
# places a real order is controlled entirely by freqtrade's OWN dry_run
# setting in freqtrade's config.json, not by this app. Check that directly
# before assuming anything is simulated. The confidence floor prefers a
# server-computed win rate (server/services/signalConfidence.ts, built from
# signal_outcomes — needs at least 8 filled-outcome fires for a pair before
# it's used) and only falls back to the weaker client-reported mlConfidence
# number when there's no server track record yet for that pair.

# ── Web Push (real notifications, even when the tab is closed) ────────────────
# Only needed if you want the 🔔 PUSH NOTIFICATIONS toggle in Settings to
# work. Generate a key pair once with: npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# On the Express server (server/.env):
#   VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=...
#   VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=...
#   VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com      # required by the push spec; any contact address
# Then also deploy the Edge Function bridge (same secrets as nexus-bot-proxy,
# no new Supabase secrets needed — reuses NEXUS_BOT_API_URL/API_AUTH_TOKEN):
#   supabase functions deploy push-proxy
# Subscriptions aren't tied to a user account — this app has no login system —
# so /api/push/send (the "Send Test Push" button) broadcasts to every
# subscribed browser. See server/migrations/004_push_subscriptions.sql.
